WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:07.310
Europa, Ganymede, Enceladus.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.563
All moons in our solar system
with one thing in common:

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.590
They're suspected to contain liquid water

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.313
below their icy surfaces.

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:17.660
And even further out,

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.680
we have some tantalizing
evidence that maybe worlds around

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.150
Neptune and potentially
even Uranus as well

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.740
could host liquid water.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.980
Even Pluto might have
a layer of liquid water

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.563
hiding somewhere
underneath that icy crust.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.093
Experts think that

00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:36.500
if there is life beyond Earth,

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.060
these massive oceans are
the place we're most likely

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.090
to find it in our solar system.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.240
NASA scientists and oceanographers

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:46.770
are working towards
missions that hopefully

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:50.450
will end up looking for
and hopefully finding

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:52.190
some forms of life,

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.550
which may be very different from ours

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.913
on those other planetary bodies.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.890
There's an increasing number of missions

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:03.910
and proposals to explore
these untapped water bodies,

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:07.570
starting with an expedition
to one of Jupiter's moons.

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:10.263
It's called the Europa Clipper Mission.

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.960
Europa Clipper Mission
is NASA's next foray

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:16.950
into the Jupiter system,

00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.830
and we're going to search
for all of these amazing

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.890
indicators of habitability and
potentially life at Europa.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:25.070
Exploring our solar system's

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:26.810
subsurface water oceans

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:30.063
is one of the next big
steps in space exploration.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:40.420
In 1977, during a mission

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.700
to photograph the barren, volcanic floor

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.700
along the mid-ocean ridge,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.500
scientists found something
that totally changed

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:50.410
our understanding of where life can exist.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.260
They collected 3,000
photos of the sea floor,

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:57.050
developed the film onboard the ship.

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.460
And as they were going
through the footage,

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.830
which was very boring

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.790
because it was just barren volcanic rock,

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.700
they came across 13 photos

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:10.413
that had pictures of large clams in them.

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:13.010
Shortly after that,

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:15.710
the deep submersible
Alvin that takes people

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:16.900
to the bottom of the ocean

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:21.530
arrived on the scene
and dived on those sites

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:24.380
and found the oases of life,

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:26.960
amidst all sorts of shimmering water

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.460
that was clearly emanating
out of the sea floor.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.120
The seawater percolating into the seafloor

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.290
was being heated by magma

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.960
and then rising to discharge
as hydrothermal vents,

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.390
and those vents were supporting

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.843
a previously unknown ecosystem.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.290
Before then, it was thought that

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:50.303
most animal life depended
on energy from the sun.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.010
If you move more than
about 50 to 100 meters

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:58.590
away from a vent, the seafloor is barren.

00:02:58.590 --> 00:02:59.810
And the question is

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.680
how do these vents support the life?

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.090
Well, clearly photosynthesis,

00:03:05.090 --> 00:03:09.140
on which most of our life
relies on our planet,

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:12.230
won't work at the bottom of
the ocean because it's dark,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.243
and so there's no sunlight,

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:19.120
so that photosynthesis
will not be operative.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.570
However, we do have these gases

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:25.320
that contain a lot of energy.

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:28.690
And because of that, there are bacteria

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:32.570
living around these areas
and other microorganisms

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:35.570
that are able to extract energy

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:38.180
from those gases

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:41.473
and use it to produce organic matter.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:47.250
And that matter feeds an array

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:48.713
of exotic animals.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:52.520
When I first saw them,

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:54.930
my mind was blown

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.780
at several things.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:58.890
One how beautiful they are,

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.760
but also how big some of the animals are.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.412
Some of the creatures
that you see down there

00:04:04.412 --> 00:04:08.660
are tube worms that can
grow 6 to 10 feet long,

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:12.240
foot-long clams, big mussels.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:15.710
And in fact, the diversity
around these vents

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:18.400
has been likened to that of the diversity

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.173
of a tropical rainforest.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:24.300
This discovery has big implications

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.200
for planetary bodies in
the universe with water,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.420
regardless of whether or
not they're close enough

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.350
to harness the energy of their host star.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.740
It suggests that there's
a possibility that

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.950
there might be life on
other planetary bodies

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:39.340
that have water.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.120
And so in some ways,

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:45.620
this discovery is helping
change our ideas about

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.113
the potential for life on other planets.

00:04:53.765 --> 00:04:54.598
In the decades

00:04:54.598 --> 00:04:57.110
since the hydrothermal vent discovery,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.258
evidence has piled up for oceans
in the outer solar system.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.520
Both the Galileo probe
that visited Jupiter

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:07.060
and the Cassini probe that visited Saturn

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:10.300
were equipped with an onboard
device called a magnetometer,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.870
which was able to give substantial

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:13.533
evidence for liquid oceans.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.900
Think of it as like a fancy compass.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.460
Basically any liquid water ocean,

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:23.990
if it has salts in it, it
can conduct electricity.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.030
And that means that if
you have a world nearby

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:30.230
like Jupiter or Saturn, that
has its own magnetic field,

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.880
they can influence each other,

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.150
and we can pick that
up with a magnetometer.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:39.490
So this is how Galileo was able to provide

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.350
tantalizing evidence
of liquid water oceans

00:05:42.350 --> 00:05:44.580
around Europa and a couple of other moons

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.063
in the Jovian system.

00:05:47.163 --> 00:05:49.840
But life, as far as we know,

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:51.013
needs more than water.

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:54.600
There needs to be an
energy source like the sun

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:58.260
or similar processes that
create hydrothermal vents.

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.210
So when the Voyager mission flew past

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:02.670
Jupiter's moon Io in 1979

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.870
and witnessed volcanic plumes,

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:07.350
scientists were thrilled to confirm

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.700
that these frozen bodies were more active

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:10.963
than previously thought.

00:06:12.230 --> 00:06:14.640
So we thought that the further
out we got from the sun,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.670
the less interesting the
solar system would be.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.580
Boy were we wrong.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:21.920
Europa is far away from the sun.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.590
It's five times farther than Earth.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:27.130
And that means that it's much colder.

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.610
Europa should be frozen
solid, but it's not.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:31.910
Why is that?

00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:34.230
Well, we think that there are two reasons.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:36.760
The first is heat that's generated by

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:39.680
radioactive elements in Europa's core.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.350
This is similar to how
Earth's interior stays

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:44.500
molten and really hot.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.440
As certain elements decay,
they give off some heat,

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.920
but we don't think that could be enough.

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.260
The other thing that we
think is keeping Europa warm

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.390
is something called tidal heating.

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:59.150
Now this happens because as
Europa orbits around Jupiter,

00:06:59.150 --> 00:07:01.430
its orbit is not a perfect circle.

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.940
Instead, it's a bit of an ellipse.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:06.820
And that means when Europa
is orbiting Jupiter,

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.060
sometimes it's a little bit closer,

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.020
sometimes it's a bit further away.

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:13.940
And so the moon actually goes like this

00:07:14.930 --> 00:07:17.390
and that title flexing generates heat

00:07:17.390 --> 00:07:20.450
just as if you were to, you
know, rub your hands together,

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:22.810
you'd generate some heat
due to that friction.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:25.220
So we think that that
also is helping to keep

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:28.050
Europa's ocean liquid
potentially for a very,

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.433
very long time.

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:32.900
Tidal heating is also the reason

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.063
for the volcanic activity
on Io observed by Voyager.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:39.420
But there are other
reasons for making Europa

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:41.340
the top priority.

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:44.400
While some moons in our solar
system have many craters,

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:45.653
Europa has few.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:50.470
And this means that the
surface is probably very young

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:53.930
due to an ocean that causes
the surface to warm and crack,

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.973
changing the landscape
and erasing craters.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.000
Based off of what we know of Europa now,

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.120
we know it has a liquid water ocean.

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.720
We know it has some building blocks

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.740
that could potentially be used

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.710
for organic molecules to make life.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:12.780
And we know it has an energy source.

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.100
So all of these lines of
evidence point towards

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.240
having the ingredients for life.

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:20.850
Not only that, but this
intense tidal heating

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:23.180
could lead to hydrothermal vents

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:25.100
down at the sea floor of Europa.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:28.110
And so this is why it's
at the top of our list

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:31.303
to study as a world that
could host life of its own.

00:08:34.290 --> 00:08:36.232
Now all we need to do is get there.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:43.110
Europa Clipper is prepared
to launch in 2024.

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:45.870
We have really amazing cameras

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.780
that are gonna take
higher-resolution images

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:49.010
than ever before.

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:52.030
We've got some imaging spectrometers.

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.780
These are instruments that
will be able to tell us

00:08:53.780 --> 00:08:56.260
what the composition of the surface is.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.260
We'll have a magnetometer,

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.210
a fancy compass to be able to understand

00:09:00.210 --> 00:09:03.030
more about Europa's interior ocean.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:05.530
We'll even have a radar sounder

00:09:05.530 --> 00:09:07.300
so that we can look for near-surface

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.290
water reservoirs too.

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:10.140
In addition to that,

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:11.730
we're going to have a few other particles

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.290
and fields instruments
to try to understand

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.080
the plasma environment.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.050
All of those ions that are zipping around

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.350
in the Jupiter system,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:20.570
we'll be able to understand those

00:09:20.570 --> 00:09:22.020
because they're important too.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:26.820
Color and infrared cameras
will map the surface

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.420
and search for hotspots where
material from within Europa,

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:33.820
possibly the ocean, may reach the surface.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:35.730
It will be the first mission sent

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:38.823
to specifically study a
moon other than our own.

00:09:39.820 --> 00:09:42.180
The mission will place
the Clipper spacecraft,

00:09:42.180 --> 00:09:44.350
which is longer than a basketball court,

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.340
in orbit around Jupiter,

00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:49.233
where it will perform
about 40 flybys of Europa.

00:09:51.310 --> 00:09:54.160
Now ideally we would love to send Clipper

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.180
and go straight into orbit around Europa,

00:09:57.180 --> 00:09:59.430
but the conditions in the Jovian system

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.220
don't make that the best option for us.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.940
There's a bit of radiation that's right in

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:08.780
and around where Europa sits
in orbit around Jupiter.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.050
And while that's not
necessarily bad for life,

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:13.260
in fact, it could make some molecules

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:15.640
and some ions that might be good for life,

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.070
it's not so good for our spacecraft.

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:21.480
So instead we're planning
on doing flybys of Europa,

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:23.400
where we go in, we get our data,

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.410
we get out, we cool off for a bit,

00:10:25.410 --> 00:10:27.750
send that data back to
Earth, and it gives us

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:30.520
as scientists plenty of
time to look over things

00:10:30.520 --> 00:10:31.853
before the next pass.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:35.450
The spacecraft may even be able to sample

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:37.793
particles high above Europa's surface.

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.370
During past Earth observations

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:44.330
and observations from the
Hubble Space Telescope,

00:10:44.330 --> 00:10:46.390
scientists have seen
something that looks like

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.990
plumes of water or other
material on Europa.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:52.240
Clipper will be equipped
with the right tools,

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:53.790
and will be able to fly close enough

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.053
to analyze those plumes
if they do indeed exist.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:01.200
This would be similar to
Saturn's moon Enceladus,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.240
where free sample from its ocean,

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:05.760
is actively venting into space.

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:07.620
Now, this would be really exciting for us

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:10.360
because some of the instruments
aboard Europa Clipper

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:13.210
could collect some of those ice grains

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:15.450
if they're there or some of the gases

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.030
and be able to tell us

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:17.990
what they're made of

00:11:17.990 --> 00:11:21.213
and help us get a glimpse of
the composition of that ocean.

00:11:25.300 --> 00:11:26.730
Although the main goal of Clipper

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.420
is to understand habitability on Europa,

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.160
not all of the questions regarding life

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:32.793
can be answered from flybys.

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.610
That's why NASA is proposing
a follow-up mission

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.550
to launch in the next couple of decades

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.020
that could land on Europa.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.010
The Europa Lander mission concept

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:45.130
is something that we've been developing

00:11:45.130 --> 00:11:48.430
that could potentially
visit a world like Europa

00:11:48.430 --> 00:11:51.440
or another ocean world one day.

00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:54.520
This concept could have some

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:57.220
type of legs to be able
to stabilize itself

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:59.570
on a really treacherous terrain

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:03.590
and a lot of engineers are
developing sampling tools

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:06.343
that could cut into very, very cold ice.

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.360
The European Space Agency

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.930
is also sending a probe in
the next couple of years

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:15.640
called the Jupiter Icy
Moons Explorer or JUICE.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:18.970
It will spend at least
three years studying Jupiter

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.110
and the habitability of
three of Jupiter's moons:

00:12:22.110 --> 00:12:26.150
Europa, Callisto and Ganymede.

00:12:26.150 --> 00:12:27.880
The last of which was recently found

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:30.773
to have possible water vapor
coming from its surface.

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:35.710
And there are even more
ambitious proposals

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.603
to explore subsurface
oceans in the far future.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.410
There are even robotic snakes.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:44.720
There's one that's called EELS,

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.450
the Exobiology Extant Life Surveyor.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.230
We love our acronyms at NASA.

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:52.350
This is something that could
slither down into cracks

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:55.870
or crevices where
biosignatures might be hiding,

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:57.850
potentially protected from radiation.

00:12:57.850 --> 00:13:00.960
So there are all sorts of
exciting concepts on the horizon.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:02.230
There are even like these hoppers

00:13:02.230 --> 00:13:04.990
that could even take
some of that water ice

00:13:04.990 --> 00:13:07.990
and then melt it and use
the steam to sort of puff

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:10.190
to bounce around, to
go to different places.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.660
Finding even microorganisms

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.430
would undoubtedly change
our understanding of life

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:20.660
and the boundaries of the habitable zone,

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.630
the area around a star which
we think could support life

00:13:23.630 --> 00:13:24.463
as we know it.

00:13:25.322 --> 00:13:27.760
Really it's caused us to change

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:31.240
our ideas for where we might look for life

00:13:31.240 --> 00:13:33.800
and how abundant habitable
conditions might be

00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:35.053
in the universe.

00:13:36.340 --> 00:13:38.300
Since many planets around other stars,

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.710
known as exoplanets,

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:42.560
are now thought to contain liquid water,

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:44.610
these expeditions may tell us more about

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.210
which of these planets
are potentially habitable

00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:50.080
and bring us closer to
answering the age-old question

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:51.233
of are we alone.

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:55.140
Ever since we, as human beings,

00:13:55.140 --> 00:13:58.920
first started to look up
and wonder about stars,

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.700
about other planets, other worlds.

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.740
We wondered if we were alone.

00:14:03.740 --> 00:14:07.723
You know, this universe is so
vast, so immense, so amazing.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.320
Are there other worlds that could be

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:13.820
more incredibly diverse
beyond our wildest dreams

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:15.570
and have life of their own?

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:18.330
Or are we just incredibly, horribly alone?

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:19.833
I hope it's the first one.

